<h2 id="dpa">11. Data processing agreement (Article 28 GDPR)</h2>
<p>This section is the data processing agreement required by Article 28(3) of Regulation (EU) 2016/679 ("GDPR"). It applies to the personal data that the user (the "controller") entrusts to Signoff (the "processor") about their clients.</p>

<h3>11.1 Subject matter, duration, nature and purpose</h3>
<p>The processor hosts and processes the data needed to send milestones to the controller's clients for approval, collect their approval or feedback, and produce approval certificates. Processing lasts as long as the user's account, plus the retention period in 11.9.</p>

<h3>11.2 Data and data subjects</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Data subjects</strong>: the controller's clients and their representatives or staff, and any person mentioned in the deliverables.</li>
  <li><strong>Categories of data</strong>: name, email address, feedback messages, IP address, browser (user agent), date and time of actions, content of deliverables.</li>
  <li>No special categories of data (Art. 9 GDPR) are expected. The controller agrees not to upload any in deliverables.</li>
</ul>

<h3>11.3 Instructions</h3>
<p>The processor processes the data only on documented instructions from the controller. These terms and the use of the service's features are those instructions. The processor immediately informs the controller if, in its opinion, an instruction infringes the GDPR or other data protection rules, or if Union or Member State law requires it to process the data, unless that law prohibits such information.</p>

<h3>11.4 Confidentiality</h3>
<p>Persons authorised to process the data are bound by confidentiality.</p>

<h3>11.5 Security</h3>
<p>The processor implements the technical and organisational measures required by Article 32 GDPR, including: encrypted connections (HTTPS), passwords stored as hashes, access control limiting each user to their own projects, protection against forged form submissions, login attempt limits, hosting in the European Union, daily backups.</p>

<h3>11.6 Sub-processors</h3>
<p>The controller gives a general authorisation to use the sub-processors listed in the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> (Render, Neon, Resend, Cloudflare). The processor informs the controller by email of any addition or replacement at least 30 days in advance; the controller may object on reasonable data protection grounds and, failing agreement, close their account. The processor imposes equivalent data protection obligations on each sub-processor by contract and remains liable for their performance.</p>

<h3>11.7 Transfers outside the European Economic Area</h3>
<p>Any transfer of data outside the EEA relies on an adequacy decision (including the EU–US Data Privacy Framework for certified providers) or on the European Commission's standard contractual clauses.</p>

<h3>11.8 Assistance</h3>
<p>Taking into account the nature of the processing, the processor assists the controller with appropriate measures: in responding to requests from data subjects exercising their rights (it forwards any request received directly without delay); in ensuring the security of processing; and, where applicable, in carrying out a data protection impact assessment and prior consultation of the supervisory authority.</p>
<p>In the event of a personal data breach, the processor notifies the controller without undue delay and at the latest <strong>48 hours</strong> after becoming aware of it, with the information available so that the controller can meet its own obligations (Articles 33 and 34 GDPR).</p>

<h3>11.9 End of processing</h3>
<p>When the account is closed, the controller may request a copy of its data. The processor then deletes the data no later than 1 year after closure, and from backups no later than 30 days after that, unless the law requires it to be kept. The controller may request earlier deletion.</p>

<h3>11.10 Audits and information</h3>
<p>The processor makes available to the controller the information needed to demonstrate compliance with Article 28 GDPR, and allows audits, including inspections, by the controller or an auditor it appoints, with reasonable notice and a confidentiality undertaking.</p>

